
  The UnixWare 7 port provides these meters:

    - load meter
    - cpu meter:  user | system | io-wait | idle
    - mem meter:  used | free
    - swap meter: used | free

  Building
  --------

    gmake

  targets/unixware builds with gcc; the UDK C driver at /usr/ccs/bin/cc
  works too if that is what is installed.  UnixWare ships no libXpm, so the
  target builds with -DNO_XPM, which drops support for the pixmapName
  resource and leaves everything else intact.

  Where the numbers come from
  ---------------------------

  Everything goes through the small interface in unixware/unixwarestats.h.
  The cpu counters and free memory come from the MAS metric file that sar(1)
  and top read, and swap from swapctl(2).  None of those need privileges.

  The load average is the exception: MAS does not carry it, so it is read
  out of /dev/kmem at the address nlist() resolves for avenrun in
  /stand/unix.  That needs root, or

    chgrp sys /usr/local/bin/xosview
    chmod 2755 /usr/local/bin/xosview

  Without it the load meter disables itself and the rest still runs.

  As on OpenServer, the kernel publishes free memory as an accumulator
  rather than a level, so the mem meter reads zero free for its first sample
  and is correct from the second on, and it cannot separate out the file
  cache.

  Untested
  --------

  This port has not yet been built or run on real hardware.  The MAS
  metrics are the part most likely to need work: the sister sng project
  reads cpu the same way on this release, but never got memory out of MAS
  there, so FREEMEM may well need a different metric id here.
